After months of on-and-off playing, I've finally achieved two
ascensions in the span of a week: lawful dwarven archeologist and
neutral human caveman.

The archeologist wasn't wishless, but might as well have been. I
wasn't really in want of anything when I got to the wand except for a
magic marker and ring of levitation, which were both promptly
destroyed (along with the wand itself) when I carelessly threw a wand
of cancellation into the bag with them before ever having used either.
Also gone was Werebane and my +5 silver saber. While scouring the
upper dungeon for a new bag of holding (which I found on my Quest) I
happened upon the portal to Fort Ludios, my first time ever going in
there. Got a fresh silver saber from an early monster drop there, and
cleared the Fort #twoweilding a +0 silver saber and a +0 blessed
thoroughly rusty thoroughly corroded pick-axe along with a ring of
conflict. Grayswandir hadn't been generated this game so I figured I
could sacrifice for it, but I'd seen so many artifacts already (most
of which exploded along with the Bag) I knew it would take thousands
of turns to see it. A couple dozen sacrifices in Quetzacoatl dropped
Demonbane on me, which was good enough.

Gehennom was uneventful, but this was only my second character to
descend that far and I'd already made one potentiall game-ending
mistake so I took it one step at a time. I was feeling good about
things until I took out Vlad and realized that literally every single
candle I had seen generated so far had gone out with the Bag. My heart
sank when I saw all the polypiling or pudding farming in my future. I
had one wand of polymorph with seven charges, that would have to do.
Very carefuly I mapped out the rest of Gehennom, marked the vibrating
square, and collected every single tool I could find. Soon I was back
in Sokoban with a mountain of unicorn horns, looking glasses, leashes
and bags. I polypiled my little heart. One of the horns fortuitously
turned into a magic marker which I stashed for a rainy day, but my
efforts only yeilded six candles. I was just about to use the marker
to write a scroll of charging for a few more poly blasts, but
something in my brain was nagging me. I had seen a candle SOMEWHERE in
the game and didn't pick it up because, at the time, I already had
seven. I decided to take one more quick pass thorugh the dungeon
before burning my marker and, sure enough, there it was laying amidst
a pile of rubbish where I had done my sacrificing.

Ascending took forever, I swear I had to walk through Orcus Town six
times. I had never been so happy to see the Valley. Planes of Earth
and Air were uneventful, but I paused on Fire. This is where my last
(and only other) ascension-worthy character had died. I knew what my
mistakes were then; I had no way of detecting the portal ("How hard
could it be?" I had thought to myself), nothing to eat ("I have a ring
of slow digestion, I'll be fine!"), and was playing carelessly because
I was so excited. This time I had all my ducks in a row and carefully
made my way to the portal, getting to Water for the first time.

Water really freaked me out (what a weird level) but I had genocided ;
already and was wearing a levitation ring so there was no real danger
to speak of. The Astral was even more exciting. I came well-equipped
with wands of teleportation, but the only wand of death I'd seen was
Orcus's, and it was empty by the time I took it. Teleportation was
enough to see me to the first altar, Death gave me no trouble, and
there before me was the high priestess of Quetzacoatl.

Grundle the Caveman was next, or perhaps Grundle the Caveman the
Second, since the First pulled a YASD when he tried to sacrifice a
werejackal and Isthar cursed his two-handed dwarven mattock. (After
playing 100+ dwarves who could safely sacrifice @, I didn't even see
this one coming.) Went from Hungry to Weak a few turns thereafter (I
was going to pray after the werejackal sacrifice to fix my hunger
issues), and with no way to reach into his bag for food died a pretty
humilating death.

Anyway, Grundle the Second had a very fortuitous early game. Found a
trident and a Hawaiian shirt in a shop on DL2 which served him well,
and more enchant weapon scrolls than he knew what to do with. Cleared
Sokoban and the Mines, blessed an identify scroll in the Minetown
sanctuary and identified every ring, scroll and wand he had found in
one go. In fact, I think every blessed id scroll I used this game gave
me a full inventory id.

Came across a gray dragon in a maze level before the Castle (which
surprised me because I didn't think dragons would be generated that
high up) who helpfullly dropped scales for me. I had yet to see an
enchant armor scroll (my shirt was still +0 even) so I just wore the
scales for now. Quest very nearly killed me (I underestimated the
Chromatic Dragon and tried to melee it), but I came to my senses and
engraved Elbereth before I could YASD. Took my Sceptre, which I
promptly corroded on a damn pudding. I didn't know artifacts could do
that. I still had lots of enchant weapon scrolls, but no good way to
confuse myself, so I read a spellbook of protection and decided to
just wait around on the quest level sacrificing until casting it would
confuse me. Probably not the best way to spend 20000 turns, but I did
get a few points of AC out of the deal.

Found Ludios again, and carted the gold back to the sanctuary for AC.
Before the Castle I had my scales, a cloak of displacement, shield of
reflection, speed boots and gauntlets of power, all +0 (the speed
boots were -1, actually, previously cursed). Still no enchant armor
scrolls. I actually used a wish on a new set of speed boots; it would
probably take two scrolls anyway to get them up to +2, so I went with
having an extra set of boots in case I did something stupid. (I
actually named cancellation wands "*** --> THIS IS CANCELLATION <--
***" this time around, and plan to do so from here on out.) Only got
four wishes total: charging scroll (only got one), speed boots, magic
marker, 2 enchant armor scrolls. I did have a magic lamp, but damned
if those things aren't more trouble than they're worth trying to get
wishes from. Much better to use them as a light source.

Decided this time to try exploring Gehennom from the ground up, so I
levelported myself to the bottom, marked the square, and worked up. It
was a nice change of pace, and I never got into trouble. Again,
though, I was short on candles. Izchak had nine for sale and I bought
them all, but I didn't put them in my bag this time because I didn't
want a repeat of my previous error. Instead I put them "somewhere
safe" in a stash somewhere, then promptly forgot where the hell my
stash was (I had gauntlets of power and a blessed bag pretty early on
and went stashless most of the game). Eventually I did find the box
and grabbed them, went for my Amulet, and cursed gain leveled myself
up. This was a lot easier than fighting my way out of the Sanctum; I'm
going to do it from now on.

Side effect of levelporting to the bottom of Gehennom: I hadn't
cleared out the Valley. Oops. Slaughtered a bunch of zombies and
ghosts and whatnot. Rodney didn't hassle me very much (I had plenty of
death wands this time) but it COULD have gotten really ugly and I was
lucky it didn't. This goes on my list of things to know for my next
ascension.

Stepped onto Earth but... no scrolls of gold detection! In fact, most
of my scrolls had been blanked. I must have fell into the water
somewhere. I remember falling in a pit and landing in Juiblex's swamp
at one point... I might have fallen into the water then scrambled out
quickly?  Is it possible to fall into a pit and land in deep water
somewhere, then drown? If so, that goes on the list. (Grundle had an
Amulet of Life Saving, but not all my characters will.) My already-
charged-once magic marker had just enough juice to write three
scrolls. I decided to go blindly on Water, because I remembered the
portal disappearing from my view anyway once the bubbles started
moving.

Almost YASDed myself on Air when I stubbornly tried to plow through an
air elemental which had engulfed me ("I have 200 HP and a -30 AC, I'll
be fine!"). I came to my senses with only 8 HP left, wand-of-deathed
the stupid thing and quaffed some full healing. I can't believe I got
so complacent so close to the goal... that's how I lost my first
almost-ascended character.

Anyway, going blindly on Water turned out to be a bad move because,
even though the portal vanished from sight last time it DID stay
remembered, so finding it wasn't troublesom. After a few hundred turns
of alternating between levitating around randomly and waiting in a
bubble for the portal to float by I finally whipped out my rainy-day
genocide scroll, got rid of ;, and put on the Amulet of Yendor to hot/
cold my way to the Astral. I remembered to switch back to Life-Saving,
but not to switch my levitation boots for speed. Dumb move, but it
didn't kill me.

Death was guarding my altar once again, first one I checked. Ascension
#2! I'm proud to have ascended two games, and proud that they were two
of the "harder" classes... but I'm most proud that I managed to avoid
the temptation of naming my caveman "Fred".

Working on my third ascension now, a monk. He's already off to a great
start (magic whistle, bag of holding and dwarvish mattock by DL3) but
great starts can be decieving. Wish me luck on #3.


